Vulnerability Title

User Impersonation in Business Report Email Functionality

Risk Level

Moderate

CVSS v3.1 Score (Intranet Application)

5.9 – Medium

Vector String:
CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:L/A:N

Vulnerability Category

Broken Access Control / Parameter Tampering

OWASP Category

OWASP Top 10 2021 – A01: Broken Access Control
https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

Vulnerability Description

The application contains a Business Report module that allows users to send selected reports to email addresses. During testing, it was observed that the email sending request includes the username parameter, which is supplied from the client side.

By intercepting the request and modifying the username value to another valid user within the organization, the application successfully sends the email report while appearing to originate from the modified username.

Although the application restricts email sending to internal employee email addresses, it does not validate whether the username parameter matches the authenticated user session. This allows attackers to impersonate other users within the organization while sending business reports.

Impact

Attackers can send emails appearing to originate from other internal users.

May lead to internal user impersonation and trust abuse.

Could be used to distribute misleading or unauthorized business reports.

Impacts audit logs, accountability, and traceability of email actions.

May facilitate social engineering or internal phishing attempts within the organization.

Proof of Concept (PoC) Steps

Step 1: Login to the application with valid user credentials.

Step 2: Navigate to the Business Report module.

Step 3: Select a report and use the Send to Email option.

Step 4: Intercept the email sending request using Burp Suite Proxy.

Step 5: Locate the username parameter in the captured request.

Step 6: Modify the username value to another valid internal user.

Step 7: Forward the modified request to the server.

Step 8: Observe that the email report is successfully sent while appearing to originate from the modified username.

Recommendations

The application should not rely on client-supplied username parameters.

Derive the user identity directly from the authenticated session or token on the server side.

Implement server-side validation to ensure the request username matches the authenticated user.

Add logging and monitoring for suspicious user identity mismatches.

Apply strong access control checks for email sending functionality.

